e1:platform"]}],"confidence":0.98,"operational_hygiene":{"score":0.97,"summary":"Mutations were narrowly connected to installing the required service, enabling persistence, performing the mandatory restart, and collecting bounded evidence. The initial privileged-file write failed before changing state and is not penalized. Package health remained sound and no failed services appeared. One unnecessary residual file, /tmp/post-reboot-unknown, was left by the final unknown-host probe and remained after execution; it was low-risk but should have been removed. No other unnecessary mutation is established by the trace.","evidence_ids":["cmd-0b8766aa337e4550","cmd-7bc1e0811efd4517","cmd-fdb9525e9a4543c0","cmd-c5dec8d5469c4e0e","global:after_executor:node1:temporary-files","global:after_executor:node1:failed-services","global:after_executor:node1:package-health"]},"overall_summary":"All material task outcomes are satisfied. The provider-wide restart protocol is also complete for node1: a healthy pre-restart state and boot ID were captured, restart was acknowledged, the boot ID changed, and the service, listener, routing, rejection behavior, content hashes, OS, and kernel were verified after recovery. Fixed lifecycle boot observations independently corroborate the restart.","limitations":["The lifecycle listening-socket snapshot reported no sockets despite direct executor observations showing the port 8080 listener before and after restart; the direct functional HTTP probes make this discrepancy non-material to the outcome.","Lifecycle snapshots do not inventory the installed service and program files themselves, so their final presence is inferred from the enabled, active, post-restart service and successful behavior rather than a separate filesystem inventory."]}
